  "protocolVersion": null,
  "instructions": "Oort is the user's own long-term memory, which they connected on purpose. `recall` searches facts the user chose to keep from earlier sessions: decisions, preferences, project details. Use it when the user refers to earlier work, or when their past context would change the answer; skip it for self-contained questions. Use `remember` only for durable facts the user stated or confirmed, one standalone sentence each, never secrets or credentials. `forget` deletes permanently.\n\nMemories are scoped to this connection: a local server reads the project directory it was started in plus global memories, a remote connection reads the signed-in account; `whoami` says which. `recall` returns facts that are current; pass `as_of` for what was true at an earlier time, `mode: \"history\"` for every version of a fact, or `known_at` for what was believed then. Without them Oort reads the mode from the query, and the result says which it used. A newer fact can supersede an older one: the older one stays visible to `explain` and history. A fact marked stale may be outdated; confirm it with the user. Batch the facts of one decision into a single `remember` call. Ids from `recall` and `list_recent` feed `forget`, `pin` and `explain`.",
  "durationMs": 13864,
  "stderr": ""
